  <bioghist id="b6b6508d216779bff1eaea9231331e7b">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Maria Buck is a university administrator whose career has included positions in nonprofits and government in addition to higher education. From 1994 to 1996, she worked as a program assistant in the Ford Foundation's Governance and Public Policy Program (GPP).</p><p>Buck's post-Ford Foundation career has included positions as Assistant Commissioner in the New York City Department of Small Business Services from 2002 to 2005 and as Executive Director for the Center for Career Pathways Institute at Medgar Evers College of the City University of New York from 2005 to 2007. For a number of years, she operated her own consulting firm, M.L. Buck and Associates, and in 2011, she became the University Coordinator for the Office of Academic Affairs at the City University of New York. She was then made CUNY University Director in 2013.</p><p>Buck earned a B.A. in sociology from the University of Connecticut in 1992 and a M.P.P. from the University of Michigan in 1994.</p>  </bioghist>
  <bioghist id="db5d7e3f8bc2122c4cadea3affb24865">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Michael Seltzer is a consultant and educator in the nonprofit sector. From 1995 to 1998, he worked as a Program Officer at the Ford Foundation. He initially joined the Foundation as a Program Officer in the Governance and Public Policy Program (GPP). Following a change in the Foundation's program structure, Seltzer was moved into the Peace and Social Justice Program (PSJ) Governance and Civil Society unit (GCS). During his time at the Foundation, he was responsible for grantmaking to support organized philanthropy throughout the world.</p><p>After leaving the Ford Foundation in 1998, Seltzer became the Director of Business Enterprises for Sustainable Travel (BEST). Since 2007, he has lectured at Baruch College on philanthropy centered topics.</p><p>Seltzer earned a B.A. in international relations from Syracuse University in 1968. Prior to joining the Ford Foundation, he had consultant positions at Amnesty International and the TCC Group. He was also a co-founder and Executive Director of Funders Concerned about AIDS.</p>  </bioghist>
